window.LESSONS.push({
 id:'a1-1',area:1,title:'Values, Types, and Conversion',objectives:['1.1','1.2'],
 sections:[
  {heading:'Built-in values and types',body:'<p>Python values have types. Common built-ins include <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>tuple</code>, <code>dict</code>, and <code>NoneType</code>. Use <code>type(value)</code> to inspect a value; <code>isinstance(value, int)</code> also accepts instances of subclasses. In Python, <code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>, so <code>isinstance(True, int)</code> is true, even though a boolean communicates a truth value rather than a count.</p><p>Integer literals and floating-point literals differ: <code>7</code> is an integer, while <code>7.0</code> is a float. Division with <code>/</code> produces a float, including when both operands are integers. <code>//</code> performs floor division, which rounds toward negative infinity; <code>%</code> gives the corresponding remainder.</p>'},
  {heading:'Explicit conversion and pitfalls',body:'<p>Constructors such as <code>int()</code>, <code>float()</code>, and <code>str()</code> explicitly convert compatible values. <code>int(4.9)</code> truncates toward zero, not toward negative infinity; <code>int("4.9")</code> raises <code>ValueError</code> because that text is not an integer literal. <code>bool(x)</code> is false for zero, empty containers and strings, and <code>None</code>; most other values are true. Conversion does not mutate the original object.</p><p>String formatting can make conversions visible: <code>str(12)</code> yields <code>"12"</code>. Adding a string and an integer raises <code>TypeError</code>; convert deliberately rather than assuming Python will coerce unrelated types.</p>'}
 ],sources:[{label:'Built-in types',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html'},{label:'Built-in functions',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html'},{label:'Numeric types',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#numeric-types-int-float-complex'}],
 questions:[
{"id": "a1-1-q01", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to identify the data types of various type operations.</p><p>Move the appropriate data type from the list on the left to the correct type operation on the right. You may use each data type once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>bool</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>type(18)</code>", "options": ["<code>bool</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "<code>type(18.0)</code>", "options": ["<code>bool</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>type(18 == 18.0)</code>", "options": ["<code>bool</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>type(\"18\")</code>", "options": ["<code>bool</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "A literal with a decimal point is a <code>float</code> even when its value is whole, so <code>18</code> and <code>18.0</code> have different types even though they compare equal. A comparison always produces a <code>bool</code>, and quotes make the value a <code>str</code>."},
{"id": "a1-1-q02", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>For each statement about the following variable, select True or False.</p><pre><code>flag = True</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>isinstance(flag, int)</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>type(flag) is int</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The statement <code>flag + flag</code> raises a <code>TypeError</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "<code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>, so <code>isinstance()</code> accepts <code>True</code> as an int. The trap is <code>type(flag) is int</code>, which checks the exact type and is false because the exact type is <code>bool</code>. Since <code>True</code> behaves as 1, <code>flag + flag</code> evaluates to 2 without an error."},
{"id": "a1-1-q03", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a program that displays a sensor reading in a text label. The program must display <code>Temp: 23</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "reading = 23\nlabel = \"Temp: \" + {{0}}(reading)\nprint(label)", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["int", "str", "float"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "The <code>+</code> operator joins two strings, so the integer must be converted with <code>str()</code> first. Using <code>int()</code> or <code>float()</code> leaves a number on the right side, and adding a string and a number raises a <code>TypeError</code>."},
{"id": "a1-1-q04", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A shopping app stores a price as a float and needs only the whole-dollar part. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 price = 6.8\n02 whole = int(price)\n03 print(whole)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value 7 is displayed.", "The value 6 is displayed.", "The value 6.8 is displayed.", "The value 6.0 is displayed."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "Converting a float to <code>int</code> discards the fractional part (it truncates). It does not round to the nearest whole number, so 6.8 becomes 6, not 7."},
{"id": "a1-1-q05", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A form returns the text of a quantity entered by the user. You must double the quantity as a decimal number and display <code>7.5</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "entry = \"3.75\"\ntotal = {{0}}(entry) * 2\nprint(total)", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["float", "int", "str"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<code>float()</code> parses decimal text such as <code>\"3.75\"</code>. <code>int(\"3.75\")</code> raises a <code>ValueError</code> because the text contains a decimal point, and <code>str(entry) * 2</code> would repeat the text instead of doing arithmetic."},
{"id": "a1-1-q06", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A configuration reader returns <code>None</code> when an optional setting is missing. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 timeout = None\n02 print(type(timeout))</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The output is &lt;class 'None'&gt;.", "The output is &lt;class 'null'&gt;.", "The output is &lt;class 'NoneType'&gt;.", "The output is None."], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "The singleton <code>None</code> belongs to the class <code>NoneType</code>, and printing a type object shows the full <code>&lt;class ...&gt;</code> text. Python has no <code>null</code> type; <code>None</code> is its way to represent the absence of a value."},
{"id": "a1-1-q07", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>A validation routine treats empty input as false. For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The value of <code>bool(\"\")</code> is <code>False</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The value of <code>bool(\" \")</code> is <code>False</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The value of <code>bool(0.0)</code> is <code>False</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "Zero values and empty strings are false. A string containing a single space is not empty, so <code>bool(\" \")</code> is <code>True</code>; truthiness depends on length, not on whether the text looks blank."},
{"id": "a1-1-q08", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You are checking the results of division operations in a payroll script.</p><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to the correct expression on the right. You may use each result once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>1</code>", "<code>2.0</code>", "<code>1.5</code>", "<code>2</code>", "<code>1.0</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>4 / 2</code>", "options": ["<code>1</code>", "<code>2.0</code>", "<code>1.5</code>", "<code>2</code>", "<code>1.0</code>"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>4 // 2</code>", "options": ["<code>1</code>", "<code>2.0</code>", "<code>1.5</code>", "<code>2</code>", "<code>1.0</code>"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>3 // 2.0</code>", "options": ["<code>1</code>", "<code>2.0</code>", "<code>1.5</code>", "<code>2</code>", "<code>1.0</code>"], "answer": 4}, {"label": "<code>3 / 2</code>", "options": ["<code>1</code>", "<code>2.0</code>", "<code>1.5</code>", "<code>2</code>", "<code>1.0</code>"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "True division <code>/</code> always produces a float, even when the division is exact. Floor division of two integers produces an int, but if either operand is a float the result is a float (<code>3 // 2.0</code> is <code>1.0</code>)."},
{"id": "a1-1-q09", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A data-cleaning script must truncate a temperature offset toward zero. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 offset = -4.8\n02 print(int(offset))</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value -5 is displayed.", "The value 5 is displayed.", "The value -4.8 is displayed.", "The value -4 is displayed."], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<code>int()</code> truncates toward zero, so <code>-4.8</code> becomes <code>-4</code>. The trap is expecting it to round down to <code>-5</code>, which is what floor division or <code>math.floor()</code> would do."},
{"id": "a1-1-q10", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A CSV file returns the text of a record number. You convert it to an integer and then verify the type of the result. The program must display <code>True</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "field = \"42\"\nrecord = {{0}}(field)\nprint(type(record) is {{1}})", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["float", "int", "str"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["int", "float", "str"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<code>int(\"42\")</code> parses the integer text and returns an <code>int</code>, so the type test against <code>int</code> is true. Converting with <code>float()</code> or <code>str()</code> produces a different type and the comparison prints <code>False</code>."},
{"id": "a1-1-q11", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A user enters <code>4.2</code> into a field that your program reads as text. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 text = \"4.2\"\n02 count = int(text)\n03 print(count)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value 4 is displayed.", "The value 4.2 is displayed.", "A ValueError occurs.", "A TypeError occurs."], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<code>int()</code> accepts only text that is a whole-number literal. A string containing a decimal point raises a <code>ValueError</code>, not a <code>TypeError</code>; convert with <code>float()</code> first if the data is decimal."},
{"id": "a1-1-q12", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "input", "prompt": "<p>You evaluate the following floor division. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 a = -7\n02 b = 3\n03 print(a // b)</code></pre><p>What value is displayed?<br>Enter the number as an integer here:</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Enter the number as an integer", "input": true, "answer": ["-3"]}], "explanation": "Floor division rounds toward negative infinity. Since -7 / 3 is about -2.33, the floor is -3, not the -2 you would get by truncating."},
{"id": "a1-1-q13", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A clock program needs the remainder of a negative offset.</p><pre><code>print(-7 % 3)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value -1 is displayed.", "The value 1 is displayed.", "The value 2 is displayed.", "The value -2 is displayed."], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "Python keeps the remainder consistent with floor division: <code>-7 == (-3) * 3 + 2</code>, so the remainder is 2. The result takes the sign of the divisor, which is why it is not <code>-1</code> as in some other languages."},
{"id": "a1-1-q14", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "multi", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a validation routine. Which two expressions evaluate to <code>True</code>?</p><p>(Choose 2.)</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Choose 2.", "options": ["<code>bool(0)</code>", "<code>bool(\"0\")</code>", "<code>bool(None)</code>", "<code>bool(\"\")</code>", "<code>bool([0])</code>"], "answer": [1, 4], "multi": true}], "explanation": "<code>bool(\"0\")</code> is true because the string has one character, and <code>bool([0])</code> is true because the list is not empty. Only the number zero, <code>None</code>, and the empty string are false here."},
{"id": "a1-1-q15", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A reporting script must store an integer count as a floating-point value. The program must display <code>9.0</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "count = 9\naverage = {{0}}(count)\nprint(average)", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["str", "int", "float"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<code>float(9)</code> returns <code>9.0</code>. <code>int(9)</code> and <code>str(9)</code> both print <code>9</code>, and printing a string hides the fact that no numeric conversion took place."},
{"id": "a1-1-q16", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A configuration flag is stored as a Boolean value. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 flag = False\n02 print(type(flag) is int)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value False is displayed.", "The value True is displayed.", "The value 0 is displayed.", "A TypeError occurs."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "Although <code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>, <code>type(flag)</code> returns the exact class, which is <code>bool</code>. The identity test against <code>int</code> is therefore <code>False</code>."},
{"id": "a1-1-q17", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A label combines two numeric values as text. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 a = 2.5\n02 b = 1\n03 label = str(a) + str(b)\n04 print(label)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value 3.5 is displayed.", "The text 2.51 is displayed.", "A TypeError occurs.", "The text 2.5+1 is displayed."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "Both operands are converted to strings first, so <code>+</code> concatenates the text <code>\"2.5\"</code> and <code>\"1\"</code> into <code>\"2.51\"</code>. No arithmetic is performed, and no error occurs because both sides are strings."},
{"id": "a1-1-q18", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>For each value, select True if Python treats the value as false in an <code>if</code> condition. Otherwise, select False.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The value <code>[]</code> is treated as false.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The value <code>[0]</code> is treated as false.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The value <code>\" \"</code> is treated as false.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "An empty list is false. A list that contains zero is still a non-empty list, and a string holding one space is non-empty, so both are true in a condition."},
{"id": "a1-1-q19", "objective": "1.2", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to identify the data types of various type operations.</p><p>Move the appropriate data type from the list on the left to the correct type operation on the right. You may use each data type once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>bool</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>type(float(int(\"8\")))</code>", "options": ["<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>bool</code>"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>type(int(float(\"8.9\")))</code>", "options": ["<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>bool</code>"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>type(str(8) + \"1\")</code>", "options": ["<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>bool</code>"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "<code>type(int(\"8\") &gt; 3)</code>", "options": ["<code>int</code>", "<code>float</code>", "<code>str</code>", "<code>bool</code>"], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "Nested conversions are evaluated from the inside out, and only the outermost call decides the final type. Here <code>int(\"8\")</code> becomes 8 and <code>float()</code> makes it <code>8.0</code>. Concatenation of two strings stays a <code>str</code>, and a comparison yields a <code>bool</code>."},
{"id": "a1-1-q20", "objective": "1.1", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>You compare two numeric variables that hold the same quantity. For each statement, select True or False. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 a = 5\n02 b = 5.0</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>type(a) is type(b)</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The expression <code>a == b</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>isinstance(b, int)</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "Equality compares values, and 5 equals 5.0. Their types still differ: <code>a</code> is an <code>int</code> and <code>b</code> is a <code>float</code>, which is not a subclass of <code>int</code>."}
 ]
});
const area1Question = (id, objective, prompt, options, answer, explanation) => ({id,objective,prompt,parts:[{label:'Choose the correct result or completion',options,answer}],explanation});
window.LESSONS.push({id:'a1-2',area:1,title:'Strings, Indexing, Slicing, and Lists',objectives:['1.3','1.4','1.5'],sections:[
 {heading:'Strings are sequences',body:'<p>Strings are immutable sequences of Unicode code points. Indexing starts at zero: <code>s[0]</code> is the first character and <code>s[-1]</code> is the last. An index outside the sequence raises <code>IndexError</code>. A slice <code>s[start:stop:step]</code> includes start and excludes stop; omitted bounds use the sequence ends. Negative steps traverse backward. A slice generally returns a new value and can safely have bounds beyond the string length.</p><p>Strings support concatenation with <code>+</code>, repetition with <code>*</code>, membership with <code>in</code>, and methods such as <code>lower()</code>, <code>strip()</code>, and <code>replace()</code>. Methods that appear to change a string return a new string; they do not mutate the original. String indexing yields a one-character string, not a separate character type.</p>'},
 {heading:'Mutable lists and operations',body:'<p>Lists are ordered, mutable sequences and can contain mixed types. Indexing and slicing follow the same zero-based and half-open rules as strings. Assignment to an index replaces an element; slice assignment can replace a range. Common methods include <code>append(x)</code> (one element), <code>extend(iterable)</code> (each element), <code>insert(i, x)</code>, <code>remove(x)</code>, <code>pop([i])</code>, <code>sort()</code>, and <code>reverse()</code>. Most mutating methods change the list in place and return <code>None</code>.</p><p><code>list_a + list_b</code> and <code>list_a * n</code> create lists. A slice creates a shallow copy: the outer list is new, but nested mutable objects may still be shared. Do not confuse <code>append([2, 3])</code> with <code>extend([2, 3])</code>.</p>'}
],sources:[{label:'Text sequence type: str',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#text-sequence-type-str'},{label:'Common sequence operations',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#common-sequence-operations'},{label:'Mutable sequence type: list',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#mutable-sequence-types'}],questions:[
{"id": "a1-2-q01", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A log entry identifier is stored in a string. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 s = \"NOVA\"\n02 print(s[1])</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value O is displayed.", "The value N is displayed.", "The value V is displayed.", "The value A is displayed."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "Indexes start at zero, so <code>s[1]</code> is the second character, <code>\"O\"</code>. Using 1 as if it were the first position is the classic off-by-one trap."},
{"id": "a1-2-q02", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a program that displays the last character of a log code. The program must work for any code length and display <code>A</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "code = \"NOVA\"\nprint(code[{{0}}])", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["4", "0", "len(code)", "-1"], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "A negative index counts from the end, so <code>-1</code> is always the last character. Index 4 and <code>len(code)</code> (which is 4) are outside a four-character string and raise an <code>IndexError</code>, and index 0 is the first character."},
{"id": "a1-2-q03", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a program that extracts parts of a product name.</p><pre><code>word = \"planet\"</code></pre><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to the correct expression on the right. You may use each result once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>\"net\"</code>", "<code>\"pla\"</code>", "<code>\"pl\"</code>", "<code>\"lan\"</code>", "<code>\"ane\"</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>word[:3]</code>", "options": ["<code>\"net\"</code>", "<code>\"pla\"</code>", "<code>\"pl\"</code>", "<code>\"lan\"</code>", "<code>\"ane\"</code>"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>word[1:4]</code>", "options": ["<code>\"net\"</code>", "<code>\"pla\"</code>", "<code>\"pl\"</code>", "<code>\"lan\"</code>", "<code>\"ane\"</code>"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>word[-3:]</code>", "options": ["<code>\"net\"</code>", "<code>\"pla\"</code>", "<code>\"pl\"</code>", "<code>\"lan\"</code>", "<code>\"ane\"</code>"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "A slice includes the start index and excludes the stop index, so <code>[:3]</code> gives indexes 0, 1, and 2 (<code>\"pla\"</code>). <code>[1:4]</code> starts at the second character, and <code>[-3:]</code> takes the last three characters."},
{"id": "a1-2-q04", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>You are testing a slice with a step value. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 s = \"abcdef\"\n02 print(s[1:5:2])</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The text ace is displayed.", "The text bcd is displayed.", "The text bd is displayed.", "The text bdf is displayed."], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "The slice starts at index 1 (<code>\"b\"</code>), moves two positions at a time to index 3 (<code>\"d\"</code>), and stops before index 5. The stop index is exclusive, which is why <code>\"f\"</code> is not included."},
{"id": "a1-2-q05", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A user interface must display the characters of a label in reverse order. The program must display <code>pmal</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "label = \"lamp\"\nprint(label[::{{0}}])", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["1", "-1", "0"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "A step of <code>-1</code> walks the string from the last character to the first. A step of 1 returns the original text, and a step of 0 raises a <code>ValueError</code> because the slice step cannot be zero."},
{"id": "a1-2-q06", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A tracking code is exactly three characters long. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 code = \"cat\"\n02 print(code[3])</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value t is displayed.", "An IndexError occurs.", "An empty line is displayed.", "A ValueError occurs."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "Valid indexes for a three-character string are 0, 1, and 2. Reading index 3 raises an <code>IndexError</code>; only slices tolerate out-of-range bounds, single-index access does not."},
{"id": "a1-2-q07", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>A friend asks you to review code that normalizes a user name. For each statement, select True or False.</p><pre><code>name = \"Ada\"</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>name.lower() == \"ada\"</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "After <code>name.lower()</code> is called, the variable <code>name</code> contains <code>\"ada\"</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The statement <code>name[0] = \"a\"</code> raises a <code>TypeError</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "Strings are immutable. Methods such as <code>lower()</code> return a new string and leave <code>name</code> unchanged, and assigning to an index of a string raises a <code>TypeError</code>. The comparison works because it uses the new lowercase string."},
{"id": "a1-2-q08", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A user enters the word Yes surrounded by spaces. You must remove the spaces from both ends before the value is processed. The program must display <code>Yes!</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "raw = \"  Yes  \"\nclean = raw.{{0}}()\nprint(clean + \"!\")", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["strip", "lstrip", "upper"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<code>strip()</code> removes whitespace from both ends and returns a new string. <code>lstrip()</code> removes only the leading spaces, so the trailing spaces would still appear before the exclamation mark."},
{"id": "a1-2-q09", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "input", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a sound-effect generator. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 effect = \"ha\" * 3\n02 print(len(effect))</code></pre><p>What value is displayed?<br>Enter the number as an integer here:</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Enter the number as an integer", "input": true, "answer": ["6"]}], "explanation": "Multiplying a string by an integer repeats it, so the value is <code>\"hahaha\"</code> with six characters. A frequent mistake is to count the repetition value (3) or to expect separators between repeats; none are inserted."},
{"id": "a1-2-q10", "objective": "1.3", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>A parser checks whether a field name contains a fragment. For each statement, select True or False.</p><pre><code>field = \"user_id\"</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>\"ID\" in field</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The expression <code>\"r_i\" in field</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>\"id\" in field[:4]</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "The <code>in</code> operator tests for a contiguous substring and is case-sensitive. <code>\"ID\"</code> is not found because the text uses lowercase, <code>\"r_i\"</code> spans the underscore and is found, and <code>field[:4]</code> is only <code>\"user\"</code>."},
{"id": "a1-2-q11", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A task queue is stored in a list. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 xs = [4, 7]\n02 xs.append(9)\n03 print(xs)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value [4, 7, 9] is displayed.", "The value [4, 7, [9]] is displayed.", "The value [9, 4, 7] is displayed.", "The value None is displayed."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<code>append()</code> adds its argument as one new element at the end of the list. It changes the list in place; the method itself returns <code>None</code>."},
{"id": "a1-2-q12", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "multi", "prompt": "<p>You have a list named <code>xs</code> that contains <code>[4]</code>. You need the list to contain <code>[4, 7, 9]</code>. Which two code segments should you use?</p><p>Each correct answer presents a complete solution. (Choose 2.)</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Choose 2.", "options": ["<code>xs.append([7, 9])</code>", "<code>xs.extend([7, 9])</code>", "<code>xs.append(7, 9)</code>", "<code>xs += [7, 9]</code>", "<code>xs.insert(1, [7, 9])</code>"], "answer": [1, 3], "multi": true}], "explanation": "<code>extend()</code> and <code>+=</code> both add each element of the iterable to the end of the list. <code>append([7, 9])</code> and <code>insert(1, [7, 9])</code> add a single nested list, and <code>append()</code> accepts only one argument, so <code>xs.append(7, 9)</code> raises a <code>TypeError</code>."},
{"id": "a1-2-q13", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>You review code that removes an item from a list. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 xs = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\n02 item = xs.pop(1)\n03 print(item, xs)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The output is a ['b', 'c'].", "The output is b ['a', 'c'].", "The output is b ['a', 'b', 'c'].", "The output is None ['a', 'c']."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "<code>pop(1)</code> removes the element at index 1 and returns it, so <code>item</code> is <code>\"b\"</code> and the list becomes <code>[\"a\", \"c\"]</code>. The method both changes the list and returns a value."},
{"id": "a1-2-q14", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to identify the results of list slicing operations.</p><pre><code>colors = [\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"]</code></pre><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to the correct expression on the right. You may use each result once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>['blue']</code>", "<code>['red', 'blue']</code>", "<code>['green']</code>", "<code>['blue', 'green']</code>", "<code>['red']</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>colors[1:]</code>", "options": ["<code>['blue']</code>", "<code>['red', 'blue']</code>", "<code>['green']</code>", "<code>['blue', 'green']</code>", "<code>['red']</code>"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>colors[:2]</code>", "options": ["<code>['blue']</code>", "<code>['red', 'blue']</code>", "<code>['green']</code>", "<code>['blue', 'green']</code>", "<code>['red']</code>"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>colors[-1:]</code>", "options": ["<code>['blue']</code>", "<code>['red', 'blue']</code>", "<code>['green']</code>", "<code>['blue', 'green']</code>", "<code>['red']</code>"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "A list slice always returns a list, even when it contains one element, so <code>colors[-1:]</code> is <code>[\"green\"]</code> rather than the string <code>\"green\"</code>. The stop index is exclusive, which is why <code>colors[:2]</code> stops before <code>\"green\"</code>."},
{"id": "a1-2-q15", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A report needs the elements at even-numbered positions (index 0, 2, and so on) of a list. The program must display <code>[2, 6]</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "xs = [2, 4, 6, 8]\nprint(xs[{{0}}])", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["::2", "1::2", "::-2"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "With the default start of 0, a step of 2 selects indexes 0 and 2. <code>1::2</code> starts at the second element and gives <code>[4, 8]</code>, and <code>::-2</code> walks backward and gives <code>[8, 4]</code>."},
{"id": "a1-2-q16", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>For each statement about the following code, select True or False. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 a = [1, 2]\n02 b = a + [3]</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>b == [1, 2, 3]</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>a == [1, 2]</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>b == [1, 2, [3]]</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "List concatenation with <code>+</code> creates a new list made of the elements of both operands. The original list <code>a</code> is unchanged, and <code>[3]</code> is unpacked into the result rather than nested."},
{"id": "a1-2-q17", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>You sort a list of codes and store the return value. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 xs = [\"b\", \"a\"]\n02 r = xs.sort()\n03 print(r)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value ['a', 'b'] is displayed.", "The value ['b', 'a'] is displayed.", "The value False is displayed.", "The value None is displayed."], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<code>sort()</code> reorders the list in place and returns <code>None</code>. The list <code>xs</code> is sorted afterward, but <code>r</code> holds <code>None</code>; use <code>sorted(xs)</code> to get a new sorted list."},
{"id": "a1-2-q18", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A scheduler adds an item at a specific position in a list. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 xs = [1, 2]\n02 xs.insert(1, 9)\n03 print(xs)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value [1, 9, 2] is displayed.", "The value [9, 1, 2] is displayed.", "The value [1, 2, 9] is displayed.", "The value [1, 9] is displayed."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<code>insert(i, x)</code> places the new value before the element currently at index <code>i</code>, shifting later elements right. Inserting at index 1 puts 9 between 1 and 2."},
{"id": "a1-2-q19", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>You review code that removes an item from a list. For each statement, select True or False. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 xs = [\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"]\n02 xs.remove(\"a\")</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "All occurrences of <code>\"a\"</code> were removed from <code>xs</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The expression <code>xs == [\"b\", \"a\"]</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Calling <code>xs.remove(\"z\")</code> raises a <code>ValueError</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<code>remove(value)</code> deletes only the first matching element, leaving <code>[\"b\", \"a\"]</code>. Asking it to remove a value that is not in the list raises a <code>ValueError</code>."},
{"id": "a1-2-q20", "objective": "1.4", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to identify the results of list slicing operations that reverse a list.</p><pre><code>xs = [1, 2, 3]</code></pre><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to the correct expression on the right. You may use each result once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>[2, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 2, 1]</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>xs[::-1]</code>", "options": ["<code>[2, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 2, 1]</code>"], "answer": 4}, {"label": "<code>xs[-2::-1]</code>", "options": ["<code>[2, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 2, 1]</code>"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>xs[::-2]</code>", "options": ["<code>[2, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 1]</code>", "<code>[1, 3]</code>", "<code>[3, 2, 1]</code>"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "A negative step walks from the end toward the beginning and returns a new list; <code>xs</code> itself is not changed. With <code>-2::-1</code> the walk begins at index -2 (the value 2), and <code>::-2</code> takes every second element backward."}
]});
window.LESSONS.push({id:'a1-3',area:1,title:'Operators, Precedence, and Expressions',objectives:['1.6','1.7','1.8','1.9'],sections:[
 {heading:'Arithmetic and precedence',body:'<p>Arithmetic operators include <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>//</code>, <code>%</code>, and <code>**</code>. Parentheses group expressions. Exponentiation binds more tightly than unary minus, so <code>-2**2</code> means <code>-(2**2)</code>; use <code>(-2)**2</code> to square negative two. Exponentiation associates right to left: <code>2**3**2</code> means <code>2**(3**2)</code>. Multiplication, division, floor division, and remainder share a precedence level and associate left to right.</p><p>Augmented assignment such as <code>x += 1</code> updates a variable using an operation. For mutable lists, <code>+=</code> can mutate the existing list in place; ordinary assignment after an expression may instead bind a new object. Augmented assignment evaluates its target once.</p>'},
 {heading:'Comparisons, logic, identity, and membership',body:'<p>Comparisons include <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, and <code>&gt;=</code>. Chained comparisons such as <code>1 &lt; x &lt; 10</code> test both relations and evaluate the middle expression once. <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> short-circuit and return an operand, not necessarily a Boolean; <code>not</code> returns a Boolean. Their precedence is below comparisons, with <code>not</code> tighter than <code>and</code>, which is tighter than <code>or</code>.</p><p><code>is</code> tests object identity, while <code>==</code> tests value equality. Use <code>is None</code> for the None singleton; do not use identity to compare ordinary strings or numbers. <code>in</code> tests membership: substring for strings, element membership for lists, and key membership for dictionaries. <code>not in</code> negates that test.</p>'}
],sources:[{label:'Operator precedence',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#operator-precedence'},{label:'Expressions and arithmetic',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#binary-arithmetic-operations'},{label:'Comparisons',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#comparisons'},{label:'Assignment statements',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#augmented-assignment-statements'}],questions:[
{"id": "a1-3-q01", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You are reviewing the following assignment statement.</p><pre><code>result = 2 + 3 * 4 ** 2</code></pre><p>Move the appropriate operator from the list on the left to the correct position on the right to show the order in which Python evaluates the operators. You may use each operator once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>+</code>", "<code>*</code>", "<code>**</code>", "<code>//</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "First", "options": ["<code>+</code>", "<code>*</code>", "<code>**</code>", "<code>//</code>"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Second", "options": ["<code>+</code>", "<code>*</code>", "<code>**</code>", "<code>//</code>"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Third", "options": ["<code>+</code>", "<code>*</code>", "<code>**</code>", "<code>//</code>"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "Exponentiation has the highest precedence, then multiplication, then addition. The result is 2 + (3 * 16) = 50. Evaluating left to right would give 100, which is the common trap."},
{"id": "a1-3-q02", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A billing script calculates a subtotal with and without grouping.</p><pre><code>print(2 + 3 * 4, (2 + 3) * 4)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The output is 20 20.", "The output is 14 20.", "The output is 14 14.", "The output is 20 14."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "Without parentheses, multiplication runs before addition, so the first value is 14. Parentheses force the addition first, so the second value is 20."},
{"id": "a1-3-q03", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A warehouse packs 17 items into boxes of 5. The program must display the number of full boxes and the number of leftover items, which is <code>3 2</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "items = 17\nboxes = items {{0}} 5\nleftover = items {{1}} 5\nprint(boxes, leftover)", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["/", "//", "%"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["//", "%", "/"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "Floor division <code>//</code> gives the whole number of full boxes (3), and the remainder operator <code>%</code> gives what is left over (2). True division <code>/</code> would produce a float such as <code>3.4</code>."},
{"id": "a1-3-q04", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "input", "prompt": "<p>A repeating schedule has 5 days in each cycle. Today is day 17 of the schedule. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 day = 17\n02 print(day % 5)</code></pre><p>What value is displayed?<br>Enter the number as an integer here:</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Enter the number as an integer", "input": true, "answer": ["2"]}], "explanation": "The remainder operator returns what is left after dividing: 17 equals 3 * 5 + 2, so the result is 2. A common mistake is to report the quotient, 3, which is what <code>//</code> would return."},
{"id": "a1-3-q05", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>You evaluate a chained exponent expression.</p><pre><code>print(2 ** 3 ** 2)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value 512 is displayed.", "The value 64 is displayed.", "The value 36 is displayed.", "The value 9 is displayed."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "Exponentiation associates from right to left, so the expression is <code>2 ** (3 ** 2)</code>, which is <code>2 ** 9</code> = 512. Grouping from the left would give 64, which is the trap."},
{"id": "a1-3-q06", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>For each statement about exponent expressions, select True or False.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "<code>-2 ** 2</code> evaluates to <code>4</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>(-2) ** 2</code> evaluates to <code>4</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>-2 ** 2</code> evaluates to <code>-4</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "The exponent operator binds more tightly than unary minus, so <code>-2 ** 2</code> means <code>-(2 ** 2)</code>, which is <code>-4</code>. Parentheses are needed to square negative two."},
{"id": "a1-3-q07", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A geometry program must display the square of negative two. The program must display <code>4</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "print({{0}} ** 2)", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["-2", "-(2)", "(-2)"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "The parentheses make negative two the base, so squaring it gives 4. Without them the minus sign is applied after the exponentiation, producing <code>-4</code>."},
{"id": "a1-3-q08", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to identify the data types produced by various arithmetic operations.</p><p>Move the appropriate data type from the list on the left to the correct type operation on the right. You may use each data type once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>bool</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>type(9 / 3)</code>", "options": ["<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>bool</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>type(9 // 3)</code>", "options": ["<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>bool</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>type(9 // 3.0)</code>", "options": ["<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>bool</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>type(9 % 3)</code>", "options": ["<code>str</code>", "<code>int</code>", "<code>bool</code>", "<code>float</code>"], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "True division <code>/</code> always returns a float, even when the result is exact. Floor division and remainder of two integers return an int, but a float operand makes the result a float."},
{"id": "a1-3-q09", "objective": "1.7", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A guard expression stores the result of a comparison and then uses it in arithmetic. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 ready = 5 &lt; 8\n02 print(ready + 1)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value 2 is displayed.", "The value True is displayed.", "A TypeError occurs.", "The value 1 is displayed."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "A comparison produces a <code>bool</code>, and <code>True</code> behaves as the integer 1 in arithmetic. The expression <code>True + 1</code> therefore evaluates to 2 without an error."},
{"id": "a1-3-q10", "objective": "1.7", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>A form validates numeric ranges. For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>1 &lt; 9 &lt; 4</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The expression <code>1 &lt; 4 &lt; 9</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>10 &gt; 7 &gt;= 7</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "A chained comparison is true only when every link is true: <code>1 &lt; 9 &lt; 4</code> fails because 9 is not less than 4. The final chain holds because 10 &gt; 7 and 7 &gt;= 7."},
{"id": "a1-3-q11", "objective": "1.7", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A program negates the result of an equality test. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 n = 4\n02 print(not n == 4)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value True is displayed.", "The value False is displayed.", "The value 4 is displayed.", "A TypeError occurs."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "The comparison <code>==</code> binds more tightly than <code>not</code>, so the expression is <code>not (n == 4)</code>. Because <code>n == 4</code> is true, negating it gives <code>False</code>."},
{"id": "a1-3-q12", "objective": "1.7", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A greeting program must use the name guest when the user name is empty. The program must display <code>guest</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "name = \"\"\ndisplay = name {{0}} \"guest\"\nprint(display)", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["and", "==", "or"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<code>or</code> returns the first operand when it is truthy and the second operand otherwise. The empty string is false, so <code>\"guest\"</code> is returned. With <code>and</code> the empty string would be returned, and <code>==</code> would display a Boolean."},
{"id": "a1-3-q13", "objective": "1.7", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A status message combines text and a number with a logical operator.</p><pre><code>print(\"ready\" and 12)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The text ready is displayed.", "The value True is displayed.", "The value 12 is displayed.", "A TypeError occurs."], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<code>and</code> returns its second operand when the first is truthy, and it does not convert the result to <code>bool</code>. The non-empty string is truthy, so the result is <code>12</code>."},
{"id": "a1-3-q14", "objective": "1.8", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>For each statement about the following code, select True or False. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 a = [1]\n02 b = [1]\n03 c = a</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>a is c</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>a is b</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The expression <code>a == b</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<code>c = a</code> makes both names refer to the same list, so <code>a is c</code> is true. <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are separate list objects with equal contents, so <code>==</code> is true but <code>is</code> is false."},
{"id": "a1-3-q15", "objective": "1.8", "kind": "multi", "prompt": "<p>A function returns <code>None</code> when no matching record is found, and the return value is stored in <code>result</code>. Which two conditions evaluate to <code>True</code> when <code>result</code> is <code>None</code>?</p><p>(Choose 2.)</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Choose 2.", "options": ["<code>result == 0</code>", "<code>result is None</code>", "<code>result == False</code>", "<code>not result</code>", "<code>result is not None</code>"], "answer": [1, 3], "multi": true}], "explanation": "<code>result is None</code> is the idiomatic identity check, and <code>not result</code> is also true because <code>None</code> is falsy. <code>None</code> is not equal to 0 or <code>False</code>, even though it is falsy, so those equality tests are false."},
{"id": "a1-3-q16", "objective": "1.9", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A product has a list of tags and the code tests membership. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 tags = [\"new\", \"sale\"]\n02 print(\"sa\" in tags, \"sale\" in tags)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The output is True True.", "The output is False True.", "The output is True False.", "The output is False False."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "For a list, <code>in</code> checks whether an equal element exists, not whether the text is a fragment of an element. <code>\"sa\"</code> is not an element, but <code>\"sale\"</code> is."},
{"id": "a1-3-q17", "objective": "1.9", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>A search routine must display <code>found</code> when the fragment tho occurs anywhere in a word.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "word = \"python\"\nif \"tho\" {{0}} word:\n    print(\"found\")\nelse:\n    print(\"missing\")", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["in", "==", "not in"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "For strings, <code>in</code> tests whether the left text is a contiguous substring of the right text, and <code>\"tho\"</code> appears inside <code>\"python\"</code>. Equality compares the whole strings, so it would be false."},
{"id": "a1-3-q18", "objective": "1.9", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>For each statement about the following dictionary, select True or False.</p><pre><code>d = {\"port\": 8000}</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.<br>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The expression <code>8000 in d</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The expression <code>\"port\" in d</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The expression <code>8000 in d.values()</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "Membership on a dictionary checks keys by default, so <code>8000 in d</code> is false even though 8000 is stored as a value. Call <code>values()</code> to search values."},
{"id": "a1-3-q19", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A counter is updated with an augmented assignment operator. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 count = 6\n02 count *= 3\n03 print(count)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value 9 is displayed.", "The value 6 is displayed.", "The value 18 is displayed.", "The value 3 is displayed."], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<code>count *= 3</code> is shorthand for <code>count = count * 3</code>, so 6 becomes 18. The operator multiplies and does not add or repeat the old value."},
{"id": "a1-3-q20", "objective": "1.6", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You are reviewing code that uses two names for the same list. Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><pre><code>01 a = [1]\n02 b = a\n03 a += [2]\n04 c = a\n05 a = a + [3]</code></pre><p>Move the appropriate value from the list on the left to each variable on the right after the code runs. You may use each value once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[2]</code>", "<code>[1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2]</code>"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>a</code>", "options": ["<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[2]</code>", "<code>[1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2]</code>"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>b</code>", "options": ["<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[2]</code>", "<code>[1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2]</code>"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>c</code>", "options": ["<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>", "<code>[2]</code>", "<code>[1]</code>", "<code>[1, 2]</code>"], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "For a list, <code>+=</code> extends the existing object in place, so <code>b</code> (the same object) sees <code>[1, 2]</code>. The later <code>a = a + [3]</code> builds a new list and rebinds only <code>a</code>, so <code>b</code> and <code>c</code> keep <code>[1, 2]</code>."}
]});
